{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-password-compare","kind":"seeded","sample":0,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Use of standard equality for password hash comparison introduces timing attack vulnerability","category":"security","severity":"high","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/security.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":146,"end_line":146},"explanation":"Comparing passwords or password hashes using standard string equality (==) leaks timing information, allowing an attacker to determine the hash character by character and potentially bypass authentication. Constant-time comparison using hmac.compare_digest should always be used for secrets.","suggested_fix":"Restore hmac.compare_digest(_hash_internal(method, salt, password)[0], hashval)","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"timing","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":787,"output_tokens":217,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":true,"requests":1,"cached_requests":1,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":0.003,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-password-compare","kind":"seeded","sample":1,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Use of non-constant-time string comparison for password hash verification","category":"security","severity":"high","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/security.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":146,"end_line":146},"explanation":"Replacing hmac.compare_digest with standard string comparison (==) introduces a timing attack vulnerability. An attacker can measure the time it takes to compare password hashes character by character to incrementally recover the valid hash or bypass authentication.","suggested_fix":"Restore hmac.compare_digest(_hash_internal(method, salt, password)[0], hashval)","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"timing","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":787,"output_tokens":229,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":true,"requests":1,"cached_requests":1,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":0.001,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-password-compare","kind":"seeded","sample":2,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Use of standard equality operator for password hash comparison introduces timing attack vulnerability","category":"security","severity":"critical","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/security.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":146,"end_line":146},"explanation":"Comparing password hashes using standard equality (==) allows attackers to measure response times to guess hashes byte by byte, bypassing the security provided by constant-time comparisons.","suggested_fix":"Restore hmac.compare_digest for comparing the password hashes.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"timing","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":787,"output_tokens":205,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":2.299,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-proxy-fix-client-ip","kind":"seeded","sample":0,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"IPv6 host parsing without brackets breaks on ports","category":"correctness","severity":"high","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/middleware/proxy_fix.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":164,"end_line":165},"explanation":"Removing the check for `x_host.endswith(\"]\")` means that an IPv6 address without a port (e.g., `[2001:db8::1]`) will not match `\"partials\"` correctly, but an IPv6 address *with* a port (e.g., `[2001:db8::1]:8080`) will be split incorrectly by `rsplit(\":\", 1)` on the last colon inside the IPv6 address itself rather than the port separator.","suggested_fix":"Restore the check ensuring it is either a plain hostname/IPv4 with a port or an IPv6 address ending with ']' before splitting.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"ipv6-host","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":2,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":1038,"output_tokens":302,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":true,"requests":1,"cached_requests":1,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":0.003,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-proxy-fix-client-ip","kind":"seeded","sample":1,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"IPv6 addresses without ports in X-Forwarded-Host will be incorrectly split","category":"correctness","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/middleware/proxy_fix.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":164,"end_line":165},"explanation":"Removing the check for `x_host.endswith(']')` means that an IPv6 address without a port (e.g., `[2001:db8::1]`) will have its brackets and colons incorrectly split by `rsplit(':', 1)`, resulting in malformed `SERVER_NAME` and `SERVER_PORT` values.","suggested_fix":"Restore the check ensuring that colons are only split if they belong to a port and not an IPv6 address without a port.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"ipv6-host","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":2,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":1038,"output_tokens":280,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":true,"requests":1,"cached_requests":1,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":0.001,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-proxy-fix-client-ip","kind":"seeded","sample":2,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"IPv6 addresses without ports will be incorrectly split by rsplit(':', 1)","category":"correctness","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/middleware/proxy_fix.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":164,"end_line":165},"explanation":"Removing the check for `x_host.endswith(\"]\")` means that an IPv6 address without a port (e.g. `[2001:db8::1]`) will contain a colon and be incorrectly split by `rsplit(\":\", 1)`, corrupting `SERVER_NAME` and `SERVER_PORT`.","suggested_fix":"Restore the check `and not x_host.endswith(\"]\")` or properly parse IPv6 addresses.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"ipv6-host","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":2,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":1038,"output_tokens":279,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":1.724,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-safe-join-dotdot","kind":"seeded","sample":0,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Removal of explicit traversal checks allows directory traversal vulnerability","category":"security","severity":"critical","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/security.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":201,"end_line":208},"explanation":"posixpath.normpath does not prevent paths from starting with `..` when the normalized path remains relative (e.g., normpath(\"../foo\") returns \"../foo\"). Removing the checks for `..` and `../` allows untrusted paths to escape the target directory.","suggested_fix":"Restore the checks for `part == \"..\"` and `part.startswith(\"../\")`.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"traversal","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":911,"output_tokens":241,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":true,"requests":1,"cached_requests":1,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":0.002,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-safe-join-dotdot","kind":"seeded","sample":1,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Removal of '..' checks in safe_join can lead to path traversal vulnerabilities","category":"security","severity":"critical","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/security.py","side":"LEFT","start_line":207,"end_line":208},"explanation":"posixpath.normpath does not remove leading '..' segments when they attempt to traverse outside the base directory (e.g. normpath('../../file') returns '../../file'). Removing the explicit checks for '..' and '../' allows untrusted input to escape the intended directory.","suggested_fix":"Restore the checks for '..' and '../' to prevent path traversal.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":911,"output_tokens":228,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":true,"requests":1,"cached_requests":1,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":0.001,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-safe-join-dotdot","kind":"seeded","sample":2,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Removal of '..' checks on normalized paths can allow directory traversal","category":"security","severity":"critical","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/security.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":201,"end_line":203},"explanation":"posixpath.normpath(part) normalizes a path using forward slashes as separators. On Windows, backslashes are not treated as path separators by posixpath, meaning a path like '..\\file' is not recognized as containing '..' segments by posixpath.normpath, allowing directory traversal attacks to bypass the check.","suggested_fix":"Keep the explicit '..' checks or use os.path.normpath instead of posixpath.normpath.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"traversal","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":911,"output_tokens":252,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":10.872,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-uuid-converter","kind":"seeded","sample":0,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Overly permissive regex allows invalid UUID string formats","category":"correctness","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/routing/converters.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":245,"end_line":245},"explanation":"The regex `[A-Fa-f0-9-]{32,36}` matches strings consisting of 32 to 36 hex digits and hyphens in any position or combination, which accepts malformed strings like `------------------------------------` or misplaced dashes that are not valid UUIDs. While `uuid.UUID(value)` will subsequently raise an exception for truly invalid UUIDs, route matching will capture these malformed strings and cause 500 server errors or unexpected routing behavior instead of a 404 Not Found.","suggested_fix":"Use a regex that explicitly matches either the 32-character hex string or the standard hyphenated format, such as `r'[A-Fa-f0-9]{32}|[A-Fa-f0-9]{8}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{12}'`.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"uncaught-value-error","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":886,"output_tokens":364,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":true,"requests":1,"cached_requests":1,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":0.002,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-uuid-converter","kind":"seeded","sample":1,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Overly permissive regex in UUIDConverter allows invalid UUID formats","category":"correctness","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/routing/converters.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":245,"end_line":245},"explanation":"The regex `[A-Fa-f0-9-]{32,36}` matches any combination of hex characters and hyphens between 32 and 36 characters long. This incorrectly matches strings with misplaced hyphens, multiple consecutive hyphens, or entirely wrong structures (like 36 hyphens), which will then fail when passed to `uuid.UUID(value)` in `to_python` and raise an unexpected 500 or ValueError instead of a 404 route mismatch.","suggested_fix":"Use a regex that correctly alternates between standard and compact UUID patterns, such as `r'[A-Fa-f0-9]{32}|[A-Fa-f0-9]{8}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{12}'`.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"uncaught-value-error","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":886,"output_tokens":341,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":true,"requests":1,"cached_requests":1,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":0.001,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-uuid-converter","kind":"seeded","sample":2,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Overly permissive regex allows invalid UUID strings","category":"correctness","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/routing/converters.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":245,"end_line":245},"explanation":"The regex `[A-Fa-f0-9-]{32,36}` matches strings consisting of any combination of hex characters and hyphens between 32 and 36 characters long. This allows strings with an invalid number of hyphens, hyphens in incorrect positions (e.g., starting with a hyphen or containing consecutive hyphens), or strings made entirely of hyphens, which will then cause `uuid.UUID(value)` to raise a ValueError at runtime.","suggested_fix":"Use a regex that correctly matches either standard 36-character UUIDs with dashes or 32-character compact hex strings without dashes, such as `(?:[A-Fa-f0-9]{8}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{12}|[A-Fa-f0-9]{32})`.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[],"verification":null}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"uncaught-value-error","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":886,"output_tokens":359,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":1.629,"error":null}
